<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<resources>
    <!-- Theme applied to the Android Window while the process is starting when the OS's Dark Mode setting is on -->
    <style name="LaunchTheme" parent="Theme.AppCompat.DayNight">
        <!-- Show a splash screen on the activity. Automatically removed when
             the Flutter engine draws its first frame -->
        <item name="android:windowBackground">@drawable/launch_background</item>
    </style>
    <!-- Theme applied to the Android Window as soon as the process has started.
         This theme determines the color of the Android Window while your
         Flutter UI initializes, as well as behind your Flutter UI while its
         running.

         This Theme is only used starting with V2 of Flutter's Android embedding. -->
    <style name="NormalTheme" parent="@android:style/Theme.Black.NoTitleBar">
        <item name="android:windowBackground">?android:colorBackground</item>
        <!-- **The three items below are correct and all three are inert on
             `targetSdk` 36. This file exists to say so, because four attempts to
             fix the black strip under the light theme all failed and the next
             person will try a fifth.**

             Measured on the Xiaomi 24090RA29G (`dumpsys window displays`):

               mNavigationBar=Window{... NavigationBar0}
                 mOwnerUid=1000  package=com.android.systemui
                 frame=[0,2556][1220,2712]  fmt=TRANSLUCENT
                 flags=[SUPPRESS_SCRIM]

             So the bottom strip is a **separate window owned by SystemUI**,
             painted on top of the app. Sampling the screenshot proves the app
             is not the one leaving it black:

               y=2650  #F2F3F0   <- the app's own page colour, Flutter's edge
               y=2690  #000000   <- SystemUI's window

             And each candidate was tested by building and sampling, not by
             reading a document:

               navigationBarColor = transparent      still #000000
               windowLightNavigationBar = true       still #000000
               windowBackground = #FF00FF            still #000000
               `cmd uimode night no` (OS light)      still #000000

             The last one is the useful one: it is not even the OS dark theme.
             MIUI draws that strip opaque whatever the app or the system says.

             `shortcut:` nothing here changes on API 36, so do not spend time
             tuning it for this device. `windowOptOutEdgeToEdgeEnforcement` is
             the one remaining app-side lever - it restores `navigationBarColor`
             by making the window stop extending behind the bar - but it also
             un-does the full-bleed app bar and `extendBodyBehindAppBar`, and it
             cannot follow an in-app theme toggle anyway. Reach for it only if a
             50 px strip is worth a layout regression. -->
        <item name="android:navigationBarColor">@android:color/transparent</item>
        <item name="android:windowLightNavigationBar">false</item>
        <item name="android:enforceNavigationBarContrast">false</item>
    </style>
</resources>